    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_24b2d77905812681a8bab240587557a36be9b66b7c511e467a46691feda0e743",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The logarithmic prime partition produces matched-variance scale increments whose two-point covariance changes with spatial separation. That covariance law supports a coarse shared-ancestry representation and, after truncated transform control and inversion, yields two complementary pair estimates: asymptotic factorization when every retained block has decorrelated and a covariance-sensitive once-versus-twice large-deviation cost for nearby points. Applying those estimates to the same grid of high-block events is what makes the second-moment existence argument work.",
