    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_b0c14e6bbb63c43f3483be765fbf29dbb6d6815d0b582902e7d526c3e4b53d87",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The same deterministic interval Sobolev inequality is reused with two different analytic inputs. For the zeta function it turns endpoint and derivative-product integrals into a coarse probabilistic upper bound for the short-interval supremum; for the mollified error zeta times M minus 1 it turns an existing mean-square approximation into uniform control throughout a typical short interval, with product differentiation generating the new derivative-moment obligations.",
